---
// Opening story for the Exemplars page: the reader decides first, then opens each outcome.
---
<section class="story-intro" aria-labelledby="story-heading">
  <div class="shell">
    <p class="eyebrow">A fictional scenario</p>
    <h2 id="story-heading">A decision on Monday morning</h2>
    <p class="story-scene">Maple Campus has no business analyst. A club organizer complains that their room was booked twice for a 60-person workshop. It is the third complaint like this since September, and Maya Chen, the manager of student services, must decide what to do.</p>

    <div class="story-decision">
      <h3>You are Maya. What do you decide?</h3>
      <ul class="decision-options">
        <li><button type="button" data-choice="A" aria-controls="instinct-outcomes" aria-pressed="false"><strong>A</strong> Buy a new online booking system. A vendor can start next month.</button></li>
        <li><button type="button" data-choice="B" aria-controls="instinct-outcomes" aria-pressed="false"><strong>B</strong> Tell Facilities to answer every request within one working day.</button></li>
        <li><button type="button" data-choice="C" aria-controls="instinct-outcomes" aria-pressed="false"><strong>C</strong> Ask the booking coordinator what went wrong. Then fix that problem.</button></li>
      </ul>
      <p>Choose an option to see what happens. Then think: what would you need to know to feel sure?</p>
    </div>

    <details class="faq-item story-reveal" id="instinct-outcomes">
      <summary>What happens when Maya decides on instinct</summary>
      <div class="story-answer">
        <p>Deciding on instinct means using experience and a quick judgment, without first checking the cause.</p>
        <ul class="story-outcomes">
          <li data-outcome="A" tabindex="-1"><span class="your-choice">Your choice</span><strong>A:</strong> Six months later, the money is spent. But requests still come by email and chat with missing details, and one coordinator still approves every booking. Confirmations are a little faster, but the complaints continue.</li>
          <li data-outcome="B" tabindex="-1"><span class="your-choice">Your choice</span><strong>B:</strong> Facilities agrees. But faster replies do not add the missing details, and nobody approves requests when the coordinator is away. After a few weeks, Facilities cannot meet the target.</li>
          <li data-outcome="C" tabindex="-1"><span class="your-choice">Your choice</span><strong>C:</strong> Maya has only 15 minutes between meetings to talk with the coordinator. The coordinator is busy and names the most recent problem: organizers forget to say how many people will attend. Maya adds a reminder to the request email. Nobody else is asked. The single approver and the calendar that staff update by hand stay hidden, and the real cause is never found.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>None of these choices are careless. Each one comes from real experience. But nobody’s job is to find out what is really causing the problem.</p>
      </div>
    </details>

    <details class="faq-item story-reveal">
      <summary>The same decision, with you as the business analyst</summary>
      <div class="story-answer">
        <p>As a business analysis professional, you first ask questions. Where do requests wait? What information is missing when they arrive? Who approves requests when the coordinator is away?</p>
        <p>You run a <a href="#facilitation">workshop</a> with organizers, Facilities and the Accessibility Office. You also study a sample of recent requests. The results point to <a href="#causes">three likely causes</a>: requests arrive incomplete from many places, one person approves everything, and staff update the calendar by hand.</p>
        <p>Maya still makes the decision. But now she approves a four-week pilot, which is a small, short trial. The pilot has <a href="#requirements">clear targets</a> that you helped set and a plan for <a href="#risks">what could go wrong</a>. If the pilot works, Maya has evidence to expand it. If it does not work, the organization loses four weeks, not the cost of a whole new system. The exemplars below show your work behind this decision.</p>
      </div>
    </details>
  </div>
</section>
<script>
  // A, B and C open the instinct outcomes and highlight the reader's choice.
  const outcomes = document.getElementById('instinct-outcomes') as HTMLDetailsElement | null;
  const buttons = document.querySelectorAll<HTMLButtonElement>('.decision-options button');
  buttons.forEach((button) => {
    button.addEventListener('click', () => {
      if (!outcomes) return;
      const choice = button.dataset.choice;
      buttons.forEach((b) => b.setAttribute('aria-pressed', String(b === button)));
      outcomes.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>('[data-outcome]').forEach((item) => {
        item.classList.toggle('is-chosen', item.dataset.outcome === choice);
      });
      outcomes.open = true;
      const chosen = outcomes.querySelector<HTMLElement>(`[data-outcome="${choice}"]`);
      const reduceMotion = window.matchMedia('(prefers-reduced-motion: reduce)').matches;
      chosen?.scrollIntoView({ behavior: reduceMotion ? 'auto' : 'smooth', block: 'center' });
      chosen?.focus({ preventScroll: true });
    });
  });
</script>
